WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
Everyone tells you the same thing: be yourself.

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:13.700
But if it's that easy, have you ever actually seen this "self" of yours — or when you strip away the masks you've layered on, will there be no face underneath at all, just emptiness?

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:21.510
And there's a tradition that says precisely the opposite: salvation lies not in being yourself, but in letting go of that "I" altogether.

4
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:40.880
Kaynak metin olarak bugünün Türkçe felsefe bölümü (`felsefe.txt`) kullanıldı; çeviri kanalı olduğu için içerik birebir korundu, hiçbir şey eklenmedi/çıkarılmadı.

5
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:49.810
Sayılar, İngilizce kanalda Türkçe okunuş zinciri atlandığı için (`ceviri_uret.py` kuralı) rakam değil yazıyla verildi.

6
00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:57.460
The most common advice offered to someone about to walk into a job interview is this: don't overthink it, just be yourself.

7
00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.430
On first hearing, it is a comforting sentence.

8
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:12.440
As though what is asked of you in that room is not a performance but a delivery; as though there is something inside you already finished, already packaged, complete, and all you have to do is set it on the table.

9
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:18.490
But slow the sentence down for a moment and lean over it: be yourself is a command.

10
00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:25.180
It is strange that it should be a command, because commands are issued only for things that might not happen on their own.

11
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.710
Nobody orders you to breathe.

12
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.160
So being yourself is not something that happens by itself.

13
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.730
It is a task that has to be carried out, one that can be missed, one that can end in failure.

14
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:46.980
What is more, the people who give this advice usually add a second sentence: but don't bring up these subjects, don't take that tone, don't sit like that.

15
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.430
In other words, be yourself, but be yourself within these limits.

16
00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:58.600
This contradiction is not a slip of the tongue; it is the everyday face of a tension that sits in the very middle of modern life.

17
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.570
The root of the problem is hidden in the oldest comparison we use.

18
00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.260
The metaphor of the mask.

19
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:09.870
We say that in company we wear masks, that we hide our real face.

20
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:16.080
We reach for this metaphor very easily, because it makes us a promise: under the mask there is a face.

21
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.930
Ready, formed, real.

22
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.580
You need only find the courage to take it off.

23
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:31.350
Even the word itself comes from the theatre; in Latin, persona was the mask an actor held to his face, and our word personality grew out of it.

24
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.000
But in the theatre there really is an actor behind the mask.

25
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.050
Is it the same in life?

26
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.700
When we take the mask off, what do we find underneath?

27
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.390
Here we should be ready for an objection.

28
00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:48.160
The call to know yourself is nothing new, you will say.

29
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:58.210
That famous phrase said to have been inscribed at the entrance of the temple of Apollo at Delphi, gnothi seauton, know thyself, is two thousand five hundred years old.

30
00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:04.260
When Socrates said that an unexamined life is not worth living, wasn't he saying the same thing?

31
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.630
No, he was not.

32
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:15.720
In the ancient world, know yourself meant, to a great extent, know your place: know that you are mortal, that you are not a god, know your limits.

33
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:24.810
And in that world the good life did not consist in expressing what was peculiar to you, but in coming closer to a shared measure of virtue proper to a human being.

34
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:33.100
For an Athenian there was no such goal as being yourself; there was being good, and the measure of the good lay not inside him but outside him.

35
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:42.350
In the same way, for a medieval craftsman the meaning of life ran not through discovering his own uniqueness but through standing, as he should, in the place he had been given.

36
00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:48.240
The idea of be yourself in the sense we understand it today begins to take shape in the eighteenth century.

37
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:58.930
In his Confessions, published after his death, Rousseau makes a surprising claim: the life of a man like himself is worth telling not because he did important things, but because he was himself.

38
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:10.260
In Rousseau's understanding of morality, what is right is told to us not by an external authority but by that unsuppressed voice within; society is the thing that pollutes that voice,

39
00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.390
that keeps making us think about how we look in other people's eyes.

40
00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:23.160
Immediately afterwards, Herder carries the idea further still: every person, every people, has a measure of its own, a way of being its own,

41
00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.050
and I can realize my own way not through imitation but only by drawing it out of myself.

42
00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:40.540
The Canadian philosopher Charles Taylor puts this transformation very plainly in his book The Ethics of Authenticity: authenticity, being true to oneself, is a moral ideal invented in the modern world.

43
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.670
Not old but new; not universal but historical.

44
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.480
And we find it as natural as water seems to a fish.

45
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:55.330
This matters, because seeing that an ideal has a history does not refute it; but it does make it open to question.

46
00:04:55.330 --> 00:05:01.380
If being yourself is a duty, that duty must have an opposite: not being yourself.

47
00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.670
So what is it like, not being yourself?

48
00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.400
How does it look?

49
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.410
Is it written on your face?

50
00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:19.180
In the middle of the twentieth century, a philosopher sitting in a café in Paris was looking at exactly this question when he was caught by the way a waiter moved.

51
00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:28.750
In Sartre's Being and Nothingness, published in nineteen forty-three, there is a scene, one of the most discussed everyday scenes in the history of philosophy.

52
00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.280
A café waiter.

53
00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:38.290
Sartre shows extraordinary attention in describing him: his movements are too quick, too precise.

54
00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:45.620
He comes up to the tables a little too nimbly, he carries the tray with a little too much skill, his voice is a little too eager.

55
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.430
Everything is in place, and that is exactly the problem.

56
00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.880
The man is not waiting tables; he is playing at being a waiter.

57
00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.570
He is looking at himself from the outside and meticulously constructing the figure that gaze expects.

58
00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.580
Sartre calls this bad faith.

59
00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:09.950
Not wickedness, not a miscalculation, but a kind of fraudulent honesty committed against oneself.

60
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:14.240
For the waiter has consented to see himself as a thing.

61
00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.370
Waiting tables has stopped being his job and become his essence.

62
00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:25.860
In this way he is relieved of a terrible burden: if I am a waiter, I do not have to get up in the morning and decide to be one.

63
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.070
The role carries me.

64
00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:32.200
Sartre's whole philosophy is built against this escape.

65
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.130
For human beings, he says, existence comes before essence.

66
00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:42.060
The essence of a knife is settled before it is made: it is designed to cut.

67
00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.550
A human being has no such design.

68
00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:49.680
First we exist, and then we build what we are out of our actions.

69
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.250
This is why, for Sartre, a human being is not what he is; he is what he is not.

70
00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:01.940
That is, at every moment he stands together with the possibility of going beyond himself, of doing something else.

71
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.750
What the waiter does is swear not to see that possibility.

72
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.600
And now the tension begins.

73
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.890
Because the same trap can be set from the opposite direction as well.

74
00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:21.180
Think of the person who takes off the mask, who refuses the role, who says, I am not going to please anyone any more, I am what I am.

75
00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.590
Very often that person says this sentence: this is how I am, I cannot help it.

76
00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.960
I am an impatient person.

77
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.290
I am a cold person.

78
00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.660
This is my nature.

79
00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:40.870
In Sartre's eyes this too is the second face of the same bad faith, more insidious, even.

80
00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:46.600
Because this time the person is hiding not behind a social role but behind a story about himself.

81
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:50.570
To say I cannot help it is to declare that you did not choose.

82
00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.220
Whereas he may be making that impatience anew each time.

83
00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.990
So taking off the mask does not guarantee finding a face underneath.

84
00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:04.560
Sometimes what lies underneath is a second mask: the mask of the real me.

85
00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:11.410
In Being and Time, published in nineteen twenty-seven, Heidegger places this situation on a wider ground.

86
00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:19.220
In his account, we spend most of our daily life inside an indefinite subject; this subject is nobody, yet it is everywhere.

87
00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:26.230
This is how it is done, this is how one dresses, this is what is expected at your age, what will people say.

88
00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.760
Heidegger calls this the they.

89
00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:33.530
The dreadful thing about the they is not that it oppresses, but that it comforts.

90
00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:39.420
If it makes the decisions, then it also answers for them; which is to say nobody answers.

91
00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:44.990
While it appears to take the burden off you, what it really does is evaporate responsibility.

92
00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:49.280
Add to this one more view, from the side of sociology.

93
00:08:49.280 --> 00:09:00.930
In The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life, published in nineteen fifty-nine, Erving Goffman analyses human relations as a stage: there is a front region, the place where we perform before an audience;

94
00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.940
and there is a back region, the wings.

95
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:11.430
We all know the relief of the wings: coming home and taking your shoes off, silencing your phone, letting your face go.

96
00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:15.560
But let us be honest, the wings are not an empty place either.

97
00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:20.490
There too we are playing a role, only with a smaller audience.

98
00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:27.340
The story we tell ourselves when we are alone also carries a narrative, a construction, a dramaturgy of its own.

99
00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:31.310
And at this point our search runs into a wall.

100
00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:38.480
We peel away layer after layer, we expect a bare core at the very bottom, and under each layer we find one more layer.

101
00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:48.210
Sartre does not flee from this; on the contrary, he accepts it: there is nothing underneath, and freedom is precisely the name of that absence.

102
00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.180
A human being is an emptiness obliged to fill itself.

103
00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:57.270
But to many people this answer comes not as a deliverance but as vertigo.

104
00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:01.080
Perhaps we are describing the thing we are looking for wrongly.

105
00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.170
Perhaps the desire to find a core at the bottom is itself the source of the real burden.

106
00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:10.460
That thought stands at the very centre of an entirely different tradition.

107
00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:16.670
In India a road was opened that runs in exactly the opposite direction from our advice to be yourself.

108
00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:23.040
The hardest principle of the Buddha's teaching to grasp is the absence of a self; in Pali, anatta.

109
00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:29.890
The claim is this: there is no fixed, indivisible I inside us that remains beneath all the changes.

110
00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:37.540
What there is, is a constantly flowing composite: body, sensations, perceptions, dispositions, consciousness.

111
00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:42.630
We gather these together and call them I, because language and habit require it.

112
00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:53.960
The most memorable telling of this idea appears in an old text that relates an imagined conversation between a ruler of Greek descent and a monk; the Milindapanha, that is, the Questions of Milinda.

113
00:10:53.960 --> 00:11:00.170
The monk Nagasena reminds King Milinda that he arrived by chariot, and asks: where is the chariot?

114
00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.220
Is the wheel the chariot?

115
00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.590
It is not.

116
00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:06.800
The axle?

117
00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.050
No.

118
00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:11.380
The seat, the yoke, the frame?

119
00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:14.070
None of them is a chariot on its own.

120
00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:19.640
And yet, when you take them all apart and lay them out on the ground, does the chariot disappear?

121
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:30.330
The lesson of this scene in the Questions of Milinda is simple and unsettling: the chariot is a real thing, but it is real not as a part; it is real as an arrangement, as a coming together.

122
00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.700
And so is the I.

123
00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.150
It is not nothing; but it is not the kind of object we take it to be.

124
00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:41.600
Now let us look at the counterpart of the same intuition in Islamic thought.

125
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:52.930
Most of us have heard those two lines of Yunus Emre, but we pass over their meaning too quickly: do not call me the me in me, I am not that either; there is an I within me, deeper in me than me.

126
00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:56.420
The I inside does not belong to the I outside.

127
00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:04.390
That is, Yunus does not count the face in the mirror, the profession, the name, the Yunus that people know, as his real self.

128
00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:15.720
To put it in the language of the Sufi tradition, the selfhood built up by the nafs is a veil; the mask that has to be removed is not the one society puts on you, but the one a person puts on himself.

129
00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.690
The most striking life story in this tradition is Gazâlî's.

130
00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:30.700
At the end of the eleventh century he was the most esteemed teacher at the Nizamiyya madrasa in Baghdad; hundreds of students attended his lectures, and he stood at the centre of the politics of his age.

131
00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:34.030
In ten ninety-five he suffered a collapse.

132
00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:45.200
In the book he later called The Deliverance from Error he describes the crisis himself: he says that he became unable to find the strength to lecture, that he could not speak, that he could not eat.

133
00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:49.650
The physicians were helpless, because the illness was not in the body.

134
00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:58.580
Gazâlî gave up his position, gave away his wealth, and wandered for years in seclusion, in Damascus, in Jerusalem, on the road to Mecca.

135
00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:05.110
What is remarkable is this: before the crisis he was already one of the most learned men of his time.

136
00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.800
What was missing was not knowledge.

137
00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:16.570
He realized that the thing he was seeking was not a doctrine to be grasped but a state to be lived, and he paid for that realization with his entire career.

138
00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:21.500
The Sufi path uses two concepts on this road: fana and baqa.

139
00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:28.030
Fana is the annihilation of selfhood; baqa is to exist, beyond it, with what is permanent.

140
00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:38.720
Mevlânâ opens the Masnavi, which he wrote in Konya in the thirteenth century, with a description of a reed: a reed cut from the reed bed can give out a sound only because it has been hollowed out.

141
00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.570
And the reason for its lament is separation.

142
00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:48.420
The metaphor is painfully clear: without being emptied you have no voice, and that emptying is a wound.

143
00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:51.910
Here we see two great roads come face to face.

144
00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:56.840
One says: to be yourself, find what is inside you and express it.

145
00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:02.570
The other says: the thing you hold on to as your self is the real obstacle, so go past it.

146
00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.900
These two roads really are opposed to each other.

147
00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:13.550
But looked at carefully, they have a common enemy: both are against the comfortable story a person builds about himself.

148
00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:17.680
Sartre's waiter took his role for his own essence.

149
00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:23.090
The selfhood Yunus criticizes takes what is passing for what is lasting.

150
00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:32.500
Both the West's ideal of authenticity and the East's path of passing beyond the self meet at the same place: the thing you think you are holding is not yours.

151
00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:35.030
This is a sorrowful discovery.

152
00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:40.920
Because a journey that set out in the hope of finding something ends in an invitation to let something go.

153
00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.170
And that invitation falls right into the middle of our most concrete decisions.

154
00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:55.420
In a lecture he gave in nineteen forty-five, later published under the title Existentialism Is a Humanism, Sartre tells of a student who came to him for advice.

155
00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.070
France is under occupation.

156
00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:04.960
The young man has no father, he lives with his mother, and his mother depends on him alone.

157
00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:12.450
He is caught between two options: stay at home and look after his mother, or join the resistance and go to the Free French Forces.

158
00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.300
Which moral rule settles that question?

159
00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:19.270
Loyalty to a mother, or loyalty to a country?

160
00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:25.960
Sartre tells his student this: no teaching, no book, no adviser can decide this in your place.

161
00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:31.210
You will choose, and with what you choose you will also have produced the kind of person you are.

162
00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:34.700
We all recognize the present-day form of this scene.

163
00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.550
The profession the family chooses.

164
00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:44.240
In Turkey, in the lives of millions of young people, this is a war compressed into a single examination day.

165
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.210
The father wants you to be a doctor, because he wants security.

166
00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:54.740
The mother points to engineering, because she wants to find for you the chance she could not find for herself.

167
00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:59.030
And the young person is trying to pick out their own voice between the two.

168
00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:03.800
It is easy to give the romantic advice here: listen to what comes from inside you.

169
00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:16.090
But after everything we have seen, we know that the voice coming from inside is not innocent either; it too may have been learned somewhere, heard somewhere, shaped by a film, by a friend, by a humiliation,

170
00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:18.300
by a word of praise.

171
00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.590
Inwardness is, very often, another guise of outwardness.

172
00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:27.040
The question has a newer form as well: the face in the profile.

173
00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:36.610
A person's social media account is the sum of frames selected from their life, and that selection is not innocent; it is disciplined by a system that hands out rewards.

174
00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:40.580
One post draws attention, and others like it multiply.

175
00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:48.390
But unlike Goffman's wings, here the front stage is permanent: it is archived, it is searchable, it can be called back.

176
00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:54.600
And the most critical point is this: the performance is watched not only by the audience but by the performer.

177
00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:59.850
A person begins to read the story about themselves that the algorithm has approved.

178
00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:09.580
In this way the call to be yourself turns into its opposite; a model of selfhood that wins approval is produced, and the person tries to resemble that model.

179
00:17:09.580 --> 00:17:15.310
Think, in fact, about where this sentence is heard most often today: in advertising.

180
00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.840
Be yourself, buy this.

181
00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:21.650
A moral ideal has been reduced to a consumer's guide.

182
00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.980
So is what we are left with a dead end?

183
00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:28.790
No, and this is exactly where the conclusion begins.

184
00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:37.560
Charles Taylor's most valuable contribution is this warning: the ideal of authenticity is not wrong, but its solitary interpretation is.

185
00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:43.610
For Taylor the human self is dialogical, that is, it is constituted in conversation.

186
00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:52.540
Every word, every concept, every moral measure we use to describe ourselves was given to us by a language, a tradition, a community.

187
00:17:52.540 --> 00:18:00.670
Being yourself, therefore, is not stripping away every bond; it is deciding which bond you will stay loyal to, and on what grounds.

188
00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:08.000
For a self to be valuable it is not enough for it to be unique; it needs a horizon beyond itself concerning what matters.

189
00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.490
Otherwise the content of uniqueness drains away.

190
00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:21.380
One of the names who approached this question most resolutely in Turkish intellectual life, Nurettin Topçu, speaks in The Ethics of Rebellion of a person becoming a personality;

191
00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:25.830
what he sets against it is not selfishness but living as part of a herd.

192
00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:34.600
The distinction here shows us the way: a personality is not someone who puts their own pleasure at the centre, but someone who takes on their own responsibility.

193
00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.610
So let us return to the question we began with.

194
00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:41.260
When you take off the mask, what is underneath?

195
00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:45.710
It was probably the wrong question, because it was looking for a face.

196
00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:51.760
Whereas all the clues we have gathered point in the same direction: the self is not an object but a verb.

197
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.570
Not something found, but something sustained.

198
00:18:55.570 --> 00:19:01.300
Like a piece of music; it is not on the sheet, it exists only as long as it is played.

199
00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:07.350
When Gazâlî began to write again he had not found the old Gazâlî; he had built a new life.

200
00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:12.440
Sartre's student, whichever he chose, would have made himself with that choice.

201
00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:18.810
And this does not make the advice to be yourself entirely meaningless; it only changes its meaning.

202
00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:27.100
It is no longer a promise implying that there is a hidden treasure inside you, but a call whose account cannot be passed on to anyone else.

203
00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:36.190
No one can choose in your place, no one can answer in your place, and whichever mask you are carrying, it is you who decides again every morning to go on carrying it.

204
00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:44.960
Perhaps that is the hardest part of it: you will not find a ready face beneath the mask, but the answer to the question of who put the mask on does not change.

205
00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:49.250
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

206
00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:52.900
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
